1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:06,279
<font color="#FFFFFF">Presented at ICML 2024, this work tackles
underwater salient instance segmentation.</font>

2
00:00:06,359 --> 00:00:08,114
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors build USIS10K,</font>

3
00:00:08,194 --> 00:00:14,326
<font color="#FFFFFF">the first large-scale underwater dataset with ten
thousand pixel-level annotated images,</font>

4
00:00:14,406 --> 00:00:15,808
<font color="#FFFFFF">and propose USIS-SAM,</font>

5
00:00:15,888 --> 00:00:21,879
<font color="#FFFFFF">a Segment Anything Model tailored to underwater
scenes that injects visual prompts and</font>

6
00:00:21,959 --> 00:00:26,820
<font color="#FFFFFF">generates salient prompts automatically to set a
new state of the art.</font>

7
00:00:27,200 --> 00:00:30,912
<font color="#FFFFFF">Underwater salient instance segmentation asks a
model to find and separate the most important objects in a scene,</font>

8
00:00:30,992 --> 00:00:34,704
<font color="#FFFFFF">a foundational step for marine exploration and robotics.</font>

9
00:00:34,784 --> 00:00:38,497
<font color="#FFFFFF">But it is hard.</font>

10
00:00:38,577 --> 00:00:42,289
<font color="#FFFFFF">Underwater images suffer light scattering, color
distortion, and marine snow,</font>

11
00:00:42,369 --> 00:00:46,081
<font color="#FFFFFF">so even top methods lose accuracy.</font>

12
00:00:46,161 --> 00:00:49,873
<font color="#FFFFFF">The field has lacked a large-scale dataset with
pixel-level salient annotations,</font>

13
00:00:49,953 --> 00:00:53,666
<font color="#FFFFFF">stalling progress.</font>

14
00:00:53,746 --> 00:00:57,458
<font color="#FFFFFF">Foundation models like SAM exist,</font>

15
00:00:57,538 --> 00:01:01,250
<font color="#FFFFFF">but were trained on natural images and struggle
underwater.</font>

16
00:01:01,630 --> 00:01:06,157
<font color="#FFFFFF">With large models advancing, SAM has spread across
computer vision,</font>

17
00:01:06,237 --> 00:01:08,632
<font color="#FFFFFF">but using it underwater falls short.</font>

18
00:01:08,712 --> 00:01:12,414
<font color="#FFFFFF">Its features do not adapt to murky, low-contrast water,</font>

19
00:01:12,494 --> 00:01:18,053
<font color="#FFFFFF">and it needs manual foreground prompts that defeat
automatic salient segmentation.</font>

20
00:01:18,133 --> 00:01:24,379
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors argue that unlocking SAM here requires
two things at once: a large-scale dataset</font>

21
00:01:24,459 --> 00:01:26,441
<font color="#FFFFFF">capturing underwater saliency,</font>

22
00:01:26,521 --> 00:01:31,461
<font color="#FFFFFF">and an architecture that teaches SAM to see
underwater and prompt itself.</font>

23
00:01:31,541 --> 00:01:33,180
<font color="#FFFFFF">This paper delivers both.</font>

24
00:01:33,560 --> 00:01:37,318
<font color="#FFFFFF">The paper makes three contributions.</font>

25
00:01:37,398 --> 00:01:41,156
<font color="#FFFFFF">First, USIS10K,</font>

26
00:01:41,236 --> 00:01:44,993
<font color="#FFFFFF">the first large-scale underwater salient instance
segmentation dataset,</font>

27
00:01:45,073 --> 00:01:48,831
<font color="#FFFFFF">with 10,632 pixel-level annotated images across
seven categories.</font>

28
00:01:48,911 --> 00:01:52,669
<font color="#FFFFFF">Second, USIS-SAM,</font>

29
00:01:52,749 --> 00:01:56,507
<font color="#FFFFFF">an architecture built on SAM featuring an
Underwater Adaptive Visual Transformer encoder</font>

30
00:01:56,587 --> 00:02:00,344
<font color="#FFFFFF">and an out-of-the-box Salient Feature Prompt
Generator that removes manual prompts.</font>

31
00:02:00,424 --> 00:02:04,182
<font color="#FFFFFF">Third, extensive experiments show it beats
state-of-the-art methods,</font>

32
00:02:04,262 --> 00:02:08,020
<font color="#FFFFFF">with data and code released.</font>

33
00:02:08,400 --> 00:02:13,844
<font color="#FFFFFF">USIS-SAM keeps SAM's pretrained backbone frozen and
adds two lightweight modules.</font>

34
00:02:13,924 --> 00:02:19,845
<font color="#FFFFFF">The Underwater Adaptive ViT encoder injects domain
knowledge through adapters: a channel</font>

35
00:02:19,925 --> 00:02:21,891
<font color="#FFFFFF">adapter recalibrates features,</font>

36
00:02:21,971 --> 00:02:27,415
<font color="#FFFFFF">while multi-scale convolutions of size three, five,
and seven capture structures,</font>

37
00:02:27,495 --> 00:02:30,551
<font color="#FFFFFF">balanced by average residuals to dampen noise.</font>

38
00:02:30,631 --> 00:02:35,666
<font color="#FFFFFF">On top, the Salient Feature Prompt Generator
replaces SAM's manual prompts.</font>

39
00:02:35,746 --> 00:02:41,735
<font color="#FFFFFF">Its Salient Feature Fusion Module aggregates
features from each UA-ViT block and produces</font>

40
00:02:41,815 --> 00:02:44,463
<font color="#FFFFFF">salient prompt embeddings automatically,</font>

41
00:02:44,543 --> 00:02:46,304
<font color="#FFFFFF">feeding SAM's mask decoder.</font>

42
00:02:46,384 --> 00:02:51,760
<font color="#FFFFFF">It trains end-to-end with a combined region,
classification, box, and mask loss.</font>

43
00:02:52,140 --> 00:02:56,184
<font color="#FFFFFF">To break the data bottleneck, the authors built USIS10K,</font>

44
00:02:56,264 --> 00:03:01,413
<font color="#FFFFFF">the first large-scale underwater salient instance
segmentation dataset.</font>

45
00:03:01,493 --> 00:03:06,715
<font color="#FFFFFF">It contains 10,632 underwater images, each with
pixel-level annotations,</font>

46
00:03:06,795 --> 00:03:10,102
<font color="#FFFFFF">spanning seven categories from diverse scenes.</font>

47
00:03:10,182 --> 00:03:14,300
<font color="#FFFFFF">The authors analyze it carefully: instances per category,</font>

48
00:03:14,380 --> 00:03:16,657
<font color="#FFFFFF">instance distribution per image,</font>

49
00:03:16,737 --> 00:03:22,106
<font color="#FFFFFF">and global versus local color contrast against the
prior SIS10K benchmark,</font>

50
00:03:22,186 --> 00:03:26,967
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing underwater imagery poses distinct
low-contrast challenges.</font>

51
00:03:27,047 --> 00:03:28,808
<font color="#FFFFFF">Beyond training USIS-SAM,</font>

52
00:03:28,888 --> 00:03:34,920
<font color="#FFFFFF">USIS10K gives the whole community a foundation for
underwater salient segmentation.</font>

53
00:03:35,300 --> 00:03:41,048
<font color="#FFFFFF">Across both settings, USIS-SAM sets a new state of
the art on USIS10K.</font>

54
00:03:41,128 --> 00:03:43,297
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the class-agnostic task,</font>

55
00:03:43,377 --> 00:03:50,207
<font color="#FFFFFF">localizing and masking salient objects regardless
of category, it reaches 59.7 mAP,</font>

56
00:03:50,287 --> 00:03:57,202
<font color="#FFFFFF">beating the best salient method OQTR by 3.1 points,
the underwater WaterMask by 0.7,</font>

57
00:03:57,282 --> 00:04:00,199
<font color="#FFFFFF">and the SAM-based RSPrompter by 1.5.</font>

58
00:04:00,279 --> 00:04:04,362
<font color="#FFFFFF">In the harder multi-class task it scores 43.1 mAP,</font>

59
00:04:04,442 --> 00:04:10,357
<font color="#FFFFFF">extending its lead to 4.4 points over WaterMask and
5.1 over RSPrompter.</font>

60
00:04:10,437 --> 00:04:15,686
<font color="#FFFFFF">It also generalizes: retrained on the land-based
SIS10K dataset,</font>

61
00:04:15,766 --> 00:04:18,850
<font color="#FFFFFF">it still outperforms prior approaches.</font>

62
00:04:19,230 --> 00:04:21,430
<font color="#FFFFFF">Ablations isolate each component.</font>

63
00:04:21,510 --> 00:04:25,922
<font color="#FFFFFF">Starting from the full model at 43.1 mAP on the
multi-class task,</font>

64
00:04:26,002 --> 00:04:32,072
<font color="#FFFFFF">reverting the Underwater Adaptive ViT block to the
original drops performance by 1.6 mAP,</font>

65
00:04:32,152 --> 00:04:37,531
<font color="#FFFFFF">confirming that adapters help the frozen backbone
handle complex marine scenes.</font>

66
00:04:37,611 --> 00:04:44,786
<font color="#FFFFFF">Replacing the Salient Feature Prompt Generator with
a generic Multi-scale Feature Enhancer costs 0.9 mAP,</font>

67
00:04:44,866 --> 00:04:50,384
<font color="#FFFFFF">showing it does more than fuse features, it focuses
attention on salient regions.</font>

68
00:04:50,464 --> 00:04:56,810
<font color="#FFFFFF">Further ablations on encoder freezing and the
convolution design confirm each choice matters.</font>

69
00:04:57,190 --> 00:05:00,473
<font color="#FFFFFF">The headline numbers tell the story.</font>

70
00:05:00,553 --> 00:05:07,385
<font color="#FFFFFF">USIS10K brings 10,632 annotated underwater images
across seven categories.</font>

71
00:05:07,465 --> 00:05:12,803
<font color="#FFFFFF">On class-agnostic segmentation, USIS-SAM reaches 59.7 mAP,</font>

72
00:05:12,883 --> 00:05:17,940
<font color="#FFFFFF">with 81.6 AP at fifty percent and 67.7 at seventy-five.</font>

73
00:05:18,020 --> 00:05:21,209
<font color="#FFFFFF">On multi-class it reaches 43.1 mAP.</font>

74
00:05:21,289 --> 00:05:31,951
<font color="#FFFFFF">Ablations attribute 1.6 mAP to the Underwater
Adaptive ViT encoder and 0.9 to the Salient Feature Prompt Generator.</font>

75
00:05:32,031 --> 00:05:38,490
<font color="#FFFFFF">Training used 24 epochs on six NVIDIA 3090 GPUs
with a ViT-H backbone.</font>

76
00:05:38,870 --> 00:05:40,357
<font color="#FFFFFF">The takeaway is simple.</font>

77
00:05:40,437 --> 00:05:46,150
<font color="#FFFFFF">Adapting a foundation model to a hard new domain
works best when you also give it the</font>

78
00:05:46,230 --> 00:05:48,603
<font color="#FFFFFF">right data and let it prompt itself.</font>

79
00:05:48,683 --> 00:05:55,145
<font color="#FFFFFF">By building USIS10K and teaching SAM to see
underwater while generating its own salient prompts,</font>

80
00:05:55,225 --> 00:05:57,666
<font color="#FFFFFF">USIS-SAM sets a new state of the art.</font>

81
00:05:57,746 --> 00:06:03,390
<font color="#FFFFFF">With dataset and code released, it lays a
foundation for underwater vision research.</font>
